          "heading": "Extended field note: why a network is more than a closed polygon",
          "content": "## Extended field note: why a network is more than a closed polygon\n\nTraverse surveying provides the computational bridge between instrument directions and mapped positions. For a north-referenced azimuth \u03b8, east increment is L sin\u03b8 and north increment is L cos\u03b8. The sign is determined by the quadrant; an angle of 120 degrees moves the point eastward and southward. When a field book mixes grid azimuth, magnetic direction and local orientation without explicit conversions, the forward calculations can be individually flawless while the final network is rotated or reflected.\n\nA closed traverse provides a useful algebraic control: the vector sum of coordinate increments should be zero after returning to the starting point, subject to measurement errors. Yet the magnitude of closure is only a diagnostic. Errors may cancel in pairs, and all points may be shifted by a common control error without affecting the closure. A second loop or external tie increases redundancy. Better network geometry, using wider azimuth diversity and avoiding unnecessarily elongated triangles, also makes it easier to distinguish different error sources.\n\nThe compass or Bowditch rule distributes north/east closure in proportion to line length. For four sides with length proportions 0.2, 0.3, 0.1 and 0.4, the first receives 20 percent of each total coordinate correction. This allocation is computationally transparent and appropriate as an illustrative approximation when observation quality is roughly comparable. It is not derived from the full covariance structure of mixed angle and distance observations. If a short line has a poorly observed angle while a long line has high-quality instruments, the length-proportional rule can conceal the actual uncertainty allocation.\n\nWeighted estimation introduces the stochastic side of network adjustment. Two independent estimates with standard deviations \u03c31 and \u03c32 can be combined using reciprocal variance weights. The combined estimate shifts toward the more precise value; the formal uncertainty shrinks if errors are independent and unbiased. But if both observations depend on one incorrect control coordinate or one shared reference station, na\u00efve independence assumptions overstate confidence. For a genuine multi-point network, least squares needs a measurement model, linearization where required, covariance treatment and statistical diagnostics. Even a small adjustment residual does not prove that the adopted datum is correct.\n\nAn area computation likewise requires geometry beyond arithmetic. The shoelace formula calculates the oriented planar area from ordered vertices, not the legal or geodesic area of land by itself. A reversed vertex order changes sign; a self-intersecting polygon can yield a deceptive algebraic sum. Test simple-polygon validity, control quality, units and projection distortion. For any official boundary report, professional authority and land records are essential evidence in addition to a coordinate-area calculation.\n\n### Capstone analysis: an urban control network with mixed reliability\n\nA redevelopment site contains two closed traverses sharing an initial monument. One loop is measured through open corridors with long high-quality EDM lines; the other crosses streets with short sights, vehicle vibration and difficult target identification. If a team blindly applies Bowditch correction to both and compares only relative closure, the mathematical treatment may conceal unequal observation quality. Plot the geometry and observation network before making any adjustment. Note which control points are shared: a common error in the initial monument can move both loops together while preserving individual closure.\n\nFor a simple illustrative 420 m loop with a 40 mm east closure, a 120 m leg receives \u221211.43 mm east correction under the length-proportional rule. This is not the standard error of the corrected point. A rigorous adjustment would use angle and distance functional equations and their uncertainty model, not just length fractions. Explain what instrument repetition, calibration, target-centering observations and redundant routes would support the weights. Compare the corrected route against a control point excluded from the adjustment so that external accuracy can be assessed separately from fit.\n\nA proposed parcel boundary is represented by a skewed quadrilateral. Before calculating its area, verify vertex order and ensure its edges do not self-intersect. For coordinates (0,0), (20,0), (24,10), (0,10), decomposition into rectangle and triangle gives 200+20=220 m\u00b2, matching the shoelace result. The agreement verifies computation for the chosen coordinates but says nothing about whether they describe a lawful land boundary. The source surveys, monuments and applicable cadastral authority still matter.\n\nThe final network memo should provide raw observations, initial orientation, east and north misclosure, adjustment model, corrected coordinates, residuals, independent control checks and legal qualifications. A result can be operationally adequate for a temporary site sketch yet inadequate for foundations or legal property definition. Separate those uses in the acceptance statement. Students should learn to prioritize the risk of a confidently wrong reference over an attractive small residual."
