          "secNumber": "5.4",
          "heading": "Extended field note: GNSS accuracy is conditional, not a display setting",
          "content": "## Extended field note: GNSS accuracy is conditional, not a display setting\n\nSatellite positioning combines ranging observations with a clock solution and a geometric model. A receiver may observe several constellations, but useful geometry and correction validity matter more than the raw number of satellites. Pseudorange observations yield a position whose quality is affected by satellite geometry, clock and orbit models, atmosphere, antenna characteristics and local reflection. Carrier-phase techniques support much finer positioning once ambiguities are properly resolved; a fixed status indicator does not remove the need for independent control checks.\n\nDilution of precision is a geometry measure. With an illustrative UERE of 0.7 m and HDOP of 1.6, a simple horizontal error scale is 1.12 m. This number is not a surveyed standard deviation for every possible receiver configuration or environment. The simplified product omits explicit non-Gaussian multipath, cycle slips, differential corrections and other factors. Two sites can have the same HDOP yet different quality because their local reflecting surfaces or obstruction patterns differ. The number of decimal places on the controller is largely a formatting choice, not a measured reliability claim.\n\nReference frames complicate apparent consistency. A plate-fixed national datum can have a different physical evolution from a global dynamic frame. Coordinates at separate epochs may differ even for a monument that has not moved relative to the ground. Earthquakes, local subsidence and structural deformation can create further site motion. Combining a 2012 control coordinate list with a 2026 receiver solution may require epoch-specific transformations or new control validation. The correct operation depends on the local authority's adopted reference realization and observed motion, not an arbitrary offset chosen until map layers appear aligned.\n\nVertical GNSS results deserve special scrutiny. Ellipsoid height is a geometric quantity, while orthometric height is related to a gravity-based vertical reference. The relation H=h\u2212N involves geoid undulation N, usually delivered through a named geoid model. For example, h=58.240 m and N=\u221217.650 m gives H=75.890 m. The sign matters. A height labelled merely \u201cMSL\u201d or \u201celevation\u201d without a named vertical datum and model is insufficient for a high-consequence floodwall, bridge or sewer invert.\n\nA prudent field plan records receiver and antenna model, antenna height method, occupation durations, correction source, solution type, base coordinates, observation epochs and rejection reasons. Use observations separated in time or changed satellite geometry, compare against independent trusted control and flag poor site visibility. Treat the GNSS receiver as an instrument in a broader quality system, not a machine that automatically knows the legally or physically correct coordinate.\n\n### Capstone analysis: GNSS control in a coastal, time-dependent landscape\n\nA planned river embankment relies on existing survey benchmarks and recent GNSS points acquired during separate field campaigns. The coastal location may be affected by subsidence and local ground movement. A responsible geodetic workflow begins with the adopted official horizontal and vertical datums and any time-dependent reference frame specifications. Do not merge coordinates from different years based on a shared place name or an apparently similar numerical range. List each observation epoch, monument condition and source realization.\n\nCompare an open-sky GNSS session against one carried out near an elevated road with multipath. Both might display favorable satellite count, yet the restricted site has reflections that distort range observations. Illustrate the geometry effect using UERE \u00d7 HDOP, then state what the model leaves out. For a given HDOP, alternative multipath conditions can still yield different positions. Record solution status, antenna-height method, correction network, base-station frame, session length and independent occupation interval. A second field visit with changed satellite geometry is often stronger evidence than an extra decimal digit.\n\nNext, convert heights only with compatible reference data. Suppose a site ellipsoid height is 58.240 m and geoid undulation is \u221217.650 m; the simplified orthometric result is 75.890 m. Show the signed arithmetic, then request the actual geoid model name and vertical datum before using that result. In a flood risk project, confusion between tidal reference levels, orthometric datum and ellipsoid heights can materially change the apparent freeboard. An independent benchmark-leveling comparison is a meaningful control on the conversion.\n\nThe deliverable should flag three different uncertainties: relative repeatability within an occupation, reference/frame consistency across campaigns, and site movement over time. A good answer should not claim a fixed position is invariant over years simply because the point label did not change. Include a conditional statement identifying the evidence needed before comparing a recent embankment top with a historical design elevation."
